#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate 200 premium Databases Q&A pairs -- Upgrade M"""
import json, pathlib

OUT = pathlib.Path('/workspace/data_upgrades')
OUT.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

pairs = [
    ("What is a database?", "A database is an organised collection of structured data stored electronically and managed by a Database Management System (DBMS). It provides persistent storage, efficient querying, concurrent access control, and data integrity guarantees. Modern databases span relational (SQL), document, key-value, column-family, graph, time-series, and vector models."),
    ("What is a relational database?", "A relational database organises data into tables (relations) of rows (tuples) and columns (attributes). Relationships between tables are expressed via foreign keys. The relational model (Codd, 1970) is based on set theory and predicate logic. SQL is the standard query language. Leading implementations: PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQL Server, SQLite."),
    ("What is SQL?", "SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language for relational databases, covering: DDL (CREATE, ALTER, DROP -- schema definition); DML (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE -- data manipulation); DCL (GRANT, REVOKE -- access control); and TCL (COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT -- transaction control). SQL is declarative -- you specify what data you want, not how to retrieve it."),
    ("What is a primary key?", "A primary key uniquely identifies each row in a table. It must be NOT NULL and unique. A table has at most one primary key (which may be composite -- multiple columns). The DBMS automatically creates a unique index on the primary key. Good primary keys: surrogate keys (auto-incremented integer or UUID) are preferred over natural keys (email, SSN) to avoid update anomalies and external coupling."),
    ("What is a foreign key?", "A foreign key is a column (or set of columns) in one table that references the primary key of another table, enforcing referential integrity. ON DELETE CASCADE propagates deletes to child rows; ON DELETE RESTRICT prevents deletion of referenced rows; ON DELETE SET NULL nullifies the FK. Foreign keys ensure that a child row cannot reference a parent row that does not exist, maintaining relational consistency."),
    ("What is normalisation?", "Normalisation is the process of organising a relational schema to reduce data redundancy and improve integrity. Normal forms: 1NF (atomic columns, no repeating groups); 2NF (1NF + no partial dependencies on a composite key); 3NF (2NF + no transitive dependencies); BCNF (every determinant is a candidate key); 4NF (no multi-valued dependencies); 5NF (no join dependencies). Practical databases are typically normalised to 3NF or BCNF."),
    ("What is first normal form?", "1NF requires: each column contains atomic (indivisible) values; each column contains values of a single type; each column has a unique name; and the order of rows and columns does not matter. Violations: repeating groups (multiple phone numbers in one column), composite values (full name in one column), and arrays stored as delimited strings. 1NF is the foundation for all higher normal forms."),
    ("What is second normal form?", "2NF requires the table to be in 1NF and every non-key attribute to be fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key (no partial dependencies). Partial dependencies only exist with composite primary keys. Example: if (order_id, product_id) is the PK but product_name depends only on product_id, that is a partial dependency. Remedy: move product_name to a Products table keyed by product_id."),
    ("What is third normal form?", "3NF requires 2NF and no transitive dependencies -- non-key attributes must not depend on other non-key attributes. Example: employee_id -> department_id -> department_name (transitive). Remedy: move department_name to a Departments table. 3NF eliminates most redundancy and is the practical target for OLTP schemas. Denormalisation (controlled violation of 3NF) is used for read-heavy analytics workloads."),
    ("What is BCNF?", "Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) requires that for every non-trivial functional dependency X -> Y, X is a superkey. BCNF is stricter than 3NF and eliminates all redundancy based on functional dependencies. Achieving BCNF may not always be possible while also preserving all functional dependencies, in which case 3NF is the practical target. Most well-designed 3NF schemas are also in BCNF."),
    ("What is a database index?", "An index is a data structure (most commonly a B-tree) that allows the DBMS to locate rows matching a predicate without scanning the entire table. It trades write overhead and storage for read speed. Types: single-column, composite (multi-column -- column order matters), unique (enforces uniqueness), partial (on a subset of rows), expression/functional, and covering (includes all queried columns -- 'index-only scan'). Over-indexing degrades write performance."),
    ("What is a B-tree index?", "A B-tree (balanced tree) index organises keys in a balanced multi-level tree where all leaf nodes are at the same depth. Internal nodes contain separator keys and pointers to child nodes; leaf nodes contain keys and row pointers (or include columns for covering indexes). B-trees support equality, range, and prefix searches in O(log n). They remain balanced via node splitting and merging on inserts/deletes. PostgreSQL and most relational DBMS use B-tree as the default index type."),
    ("What is a hash index?", "A hash index uses a hash function to map keys to bucket locations, providing O(1) average-case equality lookups. It does not support range queries, sorting, or prefix scans. It is most useful for exact-match lookups on large tables. PostgreSQL's hash indexes became crash-safe in version 10. InnoDB does not support explicit hash indexes but implements an adaptive hash index (AHI) internally in the buffer pool for hot pages."),
    ("What is a covering index?", "A covering index includes all columns needed by a query in the index itself, avoiding table lookups (heap fetches). PostgreSQL and MySQL support INCLUDE columns in indexes. Example: an index on (customer_id) INCLUDE (order_date, total) allows a query selecting order_date and total WHERE customer_id = X to be answered entirely from the index. Covering indexes are the most effective performance optimisation for read-heavy queries."),
    ("What is a composite index?", "A composite (multi-column) index covers multiple columns in a defined order. The index can be used for queries filtering on a prefix of the index columns. Rule: the leftmost prefix rule -- an index on (a, b, c) can support queries filtering on a, a+b, or a+b+c, but not b alone or b+c. Column order should match the most frequent query patterns, with the most selective or equality-filtered column first."),
    ("What is ACID?", "ACID is a set of properties guaranteeing reliable database transactions: Atomicity (all operations in a transaction succeed or all are rolled back -- no partial commits); Consistency (a transaction brings the database from one valid state to another, respecting all constraints); Isolation (concurrent transactions behave as if they were serialised -- see isolation levels); Durability (committed transactions survive system failures -- guaranteed by write-ahead logging and fsync)."),
    ("What is a transaction?", "A database transaction is a logical unit of work comprising one or more SQL operations that are treated atomically. Transactions begin explicitly (BEGIN) or implicitly, and end with COMMIT (making changes permanent) or ROLLBACK (undoing all changes). Transactions enforce ACID properties. Savepoints allow partial rollbacks within a transaction. Long-running transactions hold locks and consume resources -- they should be kept short."),
    ("What is isolation in databases?", "Transaction isolation defines how concurrent transactions see each other's uncommitted or committed changes. SQL isolation levels (ascending strictness): READ UNCOMMITTED (dirty reads allowed); READ COMMITTED (no dirty reads -- default in Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL); REPEATABLE READ (no dirty or non-repeatable reads -- default in MySQL/InnoDB); SERIALIZABLE (no dirty, non-repeatable reads, or phantom reads -- full isolation). Higher isolation reduces anomalies but increases contention."),
    ("What is a dirty read?", "A dirty read occurs when a transaction reads data written by another transaction that has not yet committed. If the writing transaction rolls back, the reader has seen data that never existed. Dirty reads violate consistency and are only permitted under READ UNCOMMITTED isolation. Practical DBMS default to at least READ COMMITTED, preventing dirty reads."),
    ("What is a phantom read?", "A phantom read occurs when a transaction re-executes a range query and sees new rows (phantoms) inserted by another committed transaction since the first read. Example: SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE age > 30 returns 5, then another transaction inserts a row age=35 and commits; the same SELECT returns 6. Phantoms are prevented by SERIALIZABLE isolation (via predicate locking or MVCC snapshot read)."),
    ("What is MVCC?", "Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) allows readers to never block writers and writers to never block readers by maintaining multiple versions of each row (with transaction timestamps). Readers see a snapshot of committed data as of their transaction start time. PostgreSQL implements MVCC via tuple visibility rules (each row version has xmin and xmax transaction IDs). InnoDB implements MVCC via undo log chains. MVCC eliminates read-write contention but requires vacuum/garbage collection of dead row versions."),
    ("What is a deadlock?", "A deadlock occurs when two or more transactions each hold a lock that the other needs, causing a circular wait from which neither can proceed. DBMS detect deadlocks by analysing the lock wait graph for cycles (cycle detection algorithms). One transaction is chosen as a victim and rolled back, releasing its locks and allowing the others to proceed. To prevent deadlocks: always acquire locks in a consistent global order; keep transactions short; use SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED where appropriate."),
    ("What is a write-ahead log?", "The Write-Ahead Log (WAL, also called the redo log or transaction log) is a durability mechanism: before any data page is modified on disk, the change is first written to the WAL. On crash recovery, the DBMS replays the WAL to restore committed changes (redo) and undo uncommitted ones (undo). WAL enables: crash recovery (durability); replication (streaming WAL to replicas); and point-in-time recovery (PITR). fsync ensures WAL reaches durable storage before COMMIT returns."),
    ("What is a checkpoint in databases?", "A checkpoint is a periodic operation that flushes dirty pages from the buffer pool to disk and records the checkpoint LSN (log sequence number) in the WAL. After a checkpoint, the DBMS only needs to replay WAL from the checkpoint LSN on crash recovery, reducing recovery time. PostgreSQL performs checkpoints every checkpoint_timeout (default 5 min) or when WAL reaches max_wal_size. Frequent checkpoints reduce recovery time but increase I/O."),
    ("What is replication in databases?", "Replication copies data from a primary (master) to one or more replicas (standbys) for high availability, read scaling, and disaster recovery. Types: synchronous (primary waits for replica to confirm write -- no data loss, higher latency); asynchronous (primary does not wait -- lower latency, potential data loss on failover). PostgreSQL streaming replication uses WAL shipping. MySQL uses binlog-based replication. Multi-master replication allows writes on multiple nodes (conflict resolution required)."),
    ("What is sharding?", "Sharding (horizontal partitioning) distributes rows of a table across multiple databases or servers (shards) based on a shard key (e.g., user_id % N). It scales write throughput and storage beyond a single server. Challenges: cross-shard queries (scatter-gather -- expensive); resharding (moving data when adding shards); hotspots (unevenly distributed keys); and loss of ACID transactions across shards. Consistent hashing minimises data movement when resharding."),
    ("What is vertical vs horizontal scaling?", "Vertical scaling (scale-up): adding CPU, RAM, or faster storage to a single server. Simple but limited by hardware maximums and expensive at the high end. Horizontal scaling (scale-out): adding more servers. Enables virtually unlimited scaling but requires the application and DBMS to distribute data and coordinate. OLTP workloads are typically vertically scaled first; OLAP and web-scale read workloads rely on horizontal scaling (read replicas, sharding, distributed databases)."),
    ("What is a NoSQL database?", "NoSQL ('Not Only SQL') databases depart from the relational model to achieve specific trade-offs in scalability, flexibility, or performance. Categories: document (MongoDB, CouchDB -- JSON-like objects with flexible schema); key-value (Redis, DynamoDB -- O(1) lookup by key); column-family (Cassandra, HBase -- wide rows, optimised for time-series and analytical workloads); graph (Neo4j, Amazon Neptune -- nodes and edges for relationship-centric data); and time-series (InfluxDB, TimescaleDB)."),
    ("What is CAP theorem?", "CAP theorem (Brewer, 2000) states that a distributed system can guarantee at most two of three properties: Consistency (all nodes see the same data at the same time); Availability (every request receives a response); Partition tolerance (the system continues operating despite network partitions). Since network partitions are unavoidable in distributed systems, the real choice is CP (consistent during partitions -- refuse requests: HBase, Zookeeper) vs AP (available during partitions -- serve possibly stale data: Cassandra, CouchDB)."),
    ("What is BASE vs ACID?", "BASE (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent) describes the properties of many NoSQL systems, contrasting with ACID. Basically Available: the system guarantees availability. Soft state: state may change over time even without input (due to eventual consistency propagation). Eventually consistent: the system will become consistent over time once updates stop propagating. BASE prioritises availability and partition tolerance over strict consistency, accepting temporary inconsistency."),
    ("What is eventual consistency?", "Eventual consistency guarantees that, if no new updates are made to a data item, all replicas will converge to the same value -- eventually. It does not guarantee when convergence occurs. Systems like DynamoDB, Cassandra, and CouchDB use eventual consistency to remain available across network partitions. Conflict resolution strategies: last-write-wins (LWW, using timestamps), vector clocks (tracking causality), and CRDTs (conflict-free replicated data types that merge deterministically)."),
    ("What is a document database?", "Document databases (MongoDB, CouchDB, Firestore) store data as semi-structured documents (JSON, BSON, XML) with flexible schemas. Documents can contain nested objects and arrays -- all related data lives together, avoiding joins. They scale horizontally. Strengths: schema flexibility, natural mapping to application objects, efficient single-document operations. Weaknesses: lack of ACID multi-document transactions (though MongoDB 4.0+ supports them), difficulty with highly relational data, and denormalisation leading to duplication."),
    ("What is MongoDB?", "MongoDB is the most popular document database. Data is stored as BSON (Binary JSON) documents in collections. Schema is flexible (documents in a collection can have different fields). Supports rich queries, aggregation pipelines, geospatial indexes, and Atlas Vector Search. Offers multi-document ACID transactions (since v4.0). Replica sets provide high availability; sharded clusters provide horizontal scaling. The aggregation pipeline ($match, $group, $project, $lookup) enables complex analytics."),
    ("What is Redis?", "Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an in-memory data structure store used as a cache, message broker, and database. Data structures: strings, lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes, streams, HyperLogLog, bitmaps, and geospatial indexes. Persistence: RDB (periodic snapshots) and AOF (append-only log). Pub/Sub for messaging. Redis Cluster provides horizontal sharding. Extremely low latency (<1 ms). Used for session caching, rate limiting, leaderboards (sorted sets), and real-time analytics."),
    ("What is Cassandra?", "Apache Cassandra is a distributed wide-column database designed for high write throughput and linear horizontal scalability. It uses a peer-to-peer (no master) architecture with consistent hashing for data distribution. The CQL (Cassandra Query Language) resembles SQL but has significant restrictions (no joins, no ad-hoc aggregation). Data model: keyspaces > tables > rows keyed by partition key (determining shard) and clustering columns (ordering within a partition). Tunable consistency (ONE, QUORUM, ALL) per operation."),
    ("What is a graph database?", "Graph databases store data as nodes (entities) and edges (relationships) with properties on both. They are optimised for traversing relationship networks: social networks (friends of friends), recommendation engines, fraud detection, and knowledge graphs. Query languages: Cypher (Neo4j), Gremlin (Apache TinkerPop), SPARQL (RDF graphs). Graph traversals that would require many expensive JOIN operations in SQL are efficient native operations in graph databases."),
    ("What is Neo4j?", "Neo4j is the most widely deployed graph database. It stores data as a property graph (nodes, relationships, and key-value properties on both). The Cypher query language is expressive: MATCH (n:Person {name:'Alice'})-[:KNOWS]->(m:Person) RETURN m.name. Neo4j supports ACID transactions. It excels at multi-hop traversals for recommendations, fraud detection, and identity resolution. Limitations: not optimised for tabular analytics; limited horizontal scalability compared to distributed SQL."),
    ("What is a time-series database?", "Time-series databases are optimised for storing and querying sequences of values indexed by time: IoT sensor readings, financial tick data, application metrics, and log data. Optimisations: time-partitioned storage (drop old partitions), columnar compression (values change slowly -- high compression ratios), efficient downsampling and interpolation, and continuous aggregation. Examples: InfluxDB (built-for-purpose), TimescaleDB (PostgreSQL extension), Prometheus (metrics), QuestDB (high-performance SQL). Key operations: range queries, downsampling, moving averages."),
    ("What is a vector database?", "Vector databases store high-dimensional vector embeddings (from ML models) and support approximate nearest neighbour (ANN) search. Used for semantic search, recommendation systems, image similarity, and RAG (retrieval-augmented generation) for LLMs. Indexing algorithms: HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World -- most popular, O(log n) search), FAISS (IVF + PQ compression), Annoy. Examples: Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant, Milvus, pgvector (PostgreSQL extension). ANN trades recall for speed -- typically >95% recall at 10-100x speed-up over exact search."),
    ("What is pgvector?", "pgvector is a PostgreSQL extension adding a vector data type and L2, inner product, and cosine distance operators and indexes (HNSW and IVFFlat). It allows semantic search, recommendation, and RAG to be built on existing PostgreSQL infrastructure without a separate vector store. Example: SELECT * FROM documents ORDER BY embedding <=> query_embedding LIMIT 5 (cosine ANN search). HNSW index in pgvector achieves >95% recall with <10 ms latency at million-scale."),
    ("What is a database schema?", "A database schema defines the structure of a database: tables, columns, data types, constraints (PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, FOREIGN KEY), and indexes. In PostgreSQL, schemas are also namespaces within a database (public schema is the default). Schema migrations (Flyway, Liquibase, Alembic, Prisma Migrate) manage schema evolution with version-controlled SQL scripts. Schema-as-code (Prisma schema, SQLAlchemy models) keeps schema definitions in application code."),
    ("What is a view in SQL?", "A view is a named SQL query stored in the database that can be queried like a table. Views simplify complex queries, enforce access control (grant access to view, not underlying tables), and provide a stable interface when the underlying schema changes. Materialised views store the query result physically and are refreshed periodically, providing fast access to expensive aggregations. PostgreSQL supports REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW (full) and CONCURRENTLY (non-blocking refresh)."),
    ("What is a stored procedure?", "A stored procedure is a named, reusable block of SQL and procedural code (PL/pgSQL, T-SQL, PL/SQL) stored in the database. Advantages: reduced network round-trips (logic executes server-side), code reuse, security (grant execute on procedure without table access), and pre-compiled execution plans. Disadvantages: business logic mixed with data layer, harder to version control, and database-vendor lock-in. Modern applications often prefer ORM or application-layer logic to stored procedures."),
    ("What is a trigger in databases?", "A trigger is a procedure automatically executed by the DBMS in response to a data modification event (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on a specified table. BEFORE triggers run before the change (can modify the row or abort the transaction); AFTER triggers run after the change; INSTEAD OF triggers replace the change (useful for updatable views). Triggers enforce complex business rules, maintain audit logs, and synchronise derived data. They add hidden complexity and should be used sparingly."),
    ("What is a CTE?", "A Common Table Expression (CTE) is a named temporary result set defined within a WITH clause that can be referenced in the main query. It improves readability of complex queries and can be recursive (WITH RECURSIVE) for traversing hierarchical or graph data (e.g., org charts, bill of materials). In PostgreSQL, CTEs are materialised by default (optimisation fence); MATERIALIZED / NOT MATERIALIZED hints control this. CTEs do not inherently improve performance."),
    ("What is a window function?", "A window function performs a calculation across a set of rows related to the current row (a window) without collapsing rows like GROUP BY. Syntax: function OVER (PARTITION BY col ORDER BY col ROWS/RANGE frame). Functions: ROW_NUMBER(), RANK(), DENSE_RANK(), NTILE(), LAG(), LEAD(), FIRST_VALUE(), LAST_VALUE(), SUM() OVER, AVG() OVER. Window functions are essential for running totals, moving averages, rankings, and year-over-year comparisons."),
    ("What is a subquery?", "A subquery is a query nested inside another SQL statement. Types: scalar subquery (returns one value -- can appear anywhere a value is expected); row subquery (returns one row); table subquery (returns a result set -- used in FROM as derived table); and correlated subquery (references columns from the outer query -- re-evaluated for each outer row). IN, EXISTS, and comparison operators can use subqueries. Subqueries are often replaceable by JOINs or CTEs for better performance."),
    ("What is the difference between INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, and FULL OUTER JOIN?", "INNER JOIN returns only rows where the join condition matches in both tables. LEFT (OUTER) JOIN returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right; unmatched right-table columns are NULL. RIGHT JOIN is the mirror. FULL OUTER JOIN returns all rows from both tables, with NULLs where no match exists. CROSS JOIN returns the Cartesian product (all combinations). SELF JOIN joins a table to itself (e.g., finding employees and their managers in the same table)."),
    ("What is query optimisation?", "The query optimiser transforms a SQL query into an efficient execution plan. Steps: parse the query (AST); transform to relational algebra; generate candidate plans (join ordering, access path selection -- index scan vs sequential scan); estimate costs using table statistics (row counts, column histograms -- ANALYZE); and select the lowest-cost plan. PostgreSQL's EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) reveals the chosen plan, actual vs estimated rows, and time per node. VACUUM and ANALYZE maintain statistics for accurate planning."),
    ("What is an execution plan?", "An execution plan is the sequence of operations the DBMS uses to execute a query. Key nodes in PostgreSQL: Seq Scan (full table scan); Index Scan (traverse B-tree, fetch heap); Index Only Scan (covering index -- no heap fetch); Bitmap Heap Scan (bulk fetch via bitmap from index); Hash Join (build hash table on smaller side, probe with larger); Merge Join (both sides sorted); Nested Loop (outer x inner -- good for small inner sets). EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows actual row counts and timings."),
    ("What is partitioning in databases?", "Table partitioning divides a large table into smaller physical sub-tables (partitions) while presenting a single logical table to queries. Types: range (by date range -- common for time-series); list (by discrete values -- region, status); hash (by hash of a column -- even distribution). Benefits: partition pruning (query only relevant partitions), faster bulk deletes (DROP PARTITION), better cache utilisation. PostgreSQL supports declarative partitioning (PARTITION BY RANGE/LIST/HASH)."),
    ("What is connection pooling?", "Database connections are expensive to create (~20 ms). Connection poolers (PgBouncer for PostgreSQL; ProxySQL for MySQL) maintain a pool of connections to the DBMS and multiplex many application connections onto them. Modes: session pooling (1:1 -- no benefit); transaction pooling (connection returned after each transaction -- most efficient); statement pooling (returned after each statement -- incompatible with prepared statements). Connection pooling is essential for high-concurrency web applications using PostgreSQL."),
    ("What is PgBouncer?", "PgBouncer is a lightweight connection pooler for PostgreSQL. In transaction pooling mode, each application connection is allocated a server connection only during an active transaction, allowing many more application connections than the PostgreSQL max_connections. It operates at the protocol level (no query parsing) with minimal overhead. Limitations: in transaction pooling mode, session-level features (LISTEN, SET, advisory locks) do not work correctly between transactions."),
    ("What is an ORM?", "An Object-Relational Mapper (ORM) maps database tables to application objects and generates SQL automatically. Examples: SQLAlchemy (Python), Hibernate (Java), ActiveRecord (Ruby on Rails), Prisma (TypeScript), GORM (Go). Benefits: reduced SQL boilerplate, database portability, automatic schema generation. Drawbacks: N+1 query problem (ORM generates one query per object in a collection -- use eager loading), loss of SQL control, and performance overhead for complex queries. Use raw SQL or query builders for performance-critical paths."),
    ("What is the N+1 query problem?", "The N+1 query problem occurs when code fetches a list of N objects (1 query) and then issues N additional queries to fetch related data for each object, instead of a single JOIN. Example: fetch 100 orders, then loop to fetch each order's customer -- 101 queries instead of 1. Solutions: eager loading (JOIN in a single query, or SELECT IN batch), DataLoader (batching in GraphQL), or raw SQL. ORMs often generate N+1 queries by default unless .includes() or .eager_load() is specified."),
    ("What is PostgreSQL?", "PostgreSQL is the most advanced open-source relational database. Features: ACID transactions with MVCC; rich data types (JSON, arrays, ranges, UUID, PostGIS geometry, vector); advanced indexing (B-tree, hash, GiST, GIN, BRIN, SP-GiST); table inheritance; foreign data wrappers (FDW); logical replication; parallel query; row-level security; and extensibility (custom types, operators, index methods, procedural languages). It is the preferred open-source database for production applications."),
    ("What is MySQL vs PostgreSQL?", "MySQL: simpler, faster for read-heavy web workloads, wide hosting support, and used heavily in LAMP stacks. PostgreSQL: more standards-compliant, richer feature set (advanced types, CTEs, window functions, FDW, PostGIS), better ACID semantics (no MyISAM-style non-transactional tables), and more extensible. MySQL's InnoDB storage engine is solid for OLTP; PostgreSQL is preferred for complex queries, analytical workloads, and GIS. Both support replication, JSON, and full-text search."),
    ("What is SQLite?", "SQLite is a serverless, file-based relational database embedded directly in the application. The entire database is a single cross-platform file. No separate server process; the database library is linked directly into the application. It is the most widely deployed database in the world (every smartphone, browser, and OS contains it). Suitable for: mobile apps, embedded systems, development, and low-concurrency desktop applications. Not suitable for: high-concurrency web apps, large datasets, or multi-server deployments."),
    ("What is a data warehouse?", "A data warehouse (DW) is a central repository of integrated historical data from multiple sources, optimised for analytical queries (OLAP). Characteristics: denormalised star or snowflake schema; columnar storage; large scans and aggregations; ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) or ELT pipelines; and slow-changing data. Examples: Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift, Databricks Lakehouse, ClickHouse, DuckDB. DWs support business intelligence (BI) tools (Tableau, Looker) for reporting and dashboards."),
    ("What is OLAP vs OLTP?", "OLTP (Online Transaction Processing): high volume of short read-write transactions, normalised schema, row-oriented storage (fast row retrieval), optimised for throughput (transactions/second). OLAP (Online Analytical Processing): complex aggregations over large datasets, denormalised star/snowflake schema, columnar storage (fast column scans and compression), optimised for latency of analytical queries. Hybrid HTAP systems (TiDB, CockroachDB, SingleStore) aim to serve both workloads."),
    ("What is columnar storage?", "Columnar (column-oriented) storage organises data by column rather than by row. Benefits for analytics: only the queried columns are read (reduced I/O); similar values compress efficiently (run-length encoding, dictionary encoding, delta encoding); and SIMD CPU vectorisation applies easily to column arrays. Columnar formats: Parquet, ORC (for data lakes); ClickHouse, BigQuery, Redshift, DuckDB (columnar databases). Row stores are better for OLTP (fetching entire rows); columnar for OLAP (scanning columns over millions of rows)."),
    ("What is a star schema?", "A star schema is a dimensional model used in data warehouses: a central fact table (containing metrics and foreign keys) surrounded by denormalised dimension tables (containing descriptive attributes). Example: a sales fact table with keys to date, customer, product, and store dimensions, plus measure columns (quantity, revenue). Simple, fast query performance (few joins), and understandable by business users. The snowflake schema normalises dimensions into sub-dimensions for reduced storage but more complex queries."),
    ("What is ETL?", "ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) is the process of moving data from source systems into a data warehouse. Extract: read data from source databases, APIs, files. Transform: clean, validate, deduplicate, aggregate, and apply business rules. Load: write transformed data into the target DW. Modern ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) loads raw data first, then transforms in the DW using SQL (enabled by scalable cloud DW compute). Tools: Apache Spark, dbt (SQL transformations), Airbyte, Fivetran, AWS Glue."),
    ("What is dbt?", "dbt (data build tool) is a transformation framework for analytics engineering. Data is loaded into the warehouse (by Airbyte, Fivetran, or custom jobs); dbt manages SQL transformations, orchestrating them as a DAG. Models are SQL SELECT statements; dbt materialises them as tables or views. Features: Jinja templating, macros, tests (not null, unique, referential integrity), documentation (auto-generated lineage graphs), and incremental models. dbt enables software engineering best practices (version control, testing) for SQL transformations."),
    ("What is Apache Spark?", "Apache Spark is an open-source distributed computing framework for large-scale data processing. Core abstraction: RDD (Resilient Distributed Dataset -- fault-tolerant, partitioned collections); higher-level APIs: DataFrames (tabular, Catalyst query optimiser) and Datasets (type-safe). Modules: Spark SQL, Spark Streaming (micro-batch and structured streaming), MLlib, GraphX. Spark processes data in memory (10-100x faster than MapReduce for iterative algorithms). PySpark is the Python API."),
    ("What is Hadoop?", "Apache Hadoop is an open-source framework for distributed storage (HDFS -- Hadoop Distributed File System) and processing (originally MapReduce) of large datasets on commodity hardware. HDFS replicates data blocks (default 3x) across nodes for fault tolerance. MapReduce (now largely replaced by Spark) executes jobs as map (parallel transform) and reduce (aggregation) phases. Hadoop ecosystem: Hive (SQL on HDFS), HBase (columnar store on HDFS), Pig, Oozie, YARN (resource manager)."),
    ("What is a data lake?", "A data lake stores raw data at any scale in its native format (structured, semi-structured, unstructured) in cheap object storage (S3, GCS, ADLS). Unlike data warehouses, data lakes impose schema on read. The lakehouse architecture (Databricks Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Apache Hudi) adds ACID transactions, schema enforcement, time-travel, and efficient incremental processing to data lakes, combining the scalability of lakes with the reliability of warehouses."),
    ("What is Apache Parquet?", "Parquet is an open-source columnar file format for big data analytics. Features: nested data structures (Dremel encoding); efficient compression (Snappy, Zstd, Gzip); column statistics (min/max, null count) enabling predicate pushdown; and support for complex types. It is the de facto standard for storing structured data in data lakes (S3 + Parquet + Spark/DuckDB). Compared to CSV: 10-100x smaller, 10-100x faster for analytical queries. Apache ORC is a similar format optimised for Hive."),
    ("What is Apache Kafka?", "Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform for high-throughput, low-latency data pipelines. Concepts: producers publish messages to topics; topics are divided into partitions (ordered, immutable log -- messages are append-only); consumers subscribe to topics (consumer groups for parallel consumption). Brokers form a cluster; replication ensures durability. Messages are retained for a configurable period. Kafka supports exactly-once semantics (EOS), real-time stream processing (Kafka Streams, ksqlDB), and connects to databases (Kafka Connect with Debezium for CDC)."),
    ("What is change data capture?", "Change data capture (CDC) captures row-level changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) from a source database in real time without impacting the database. Mechanisms: log-based CDC (reads the database WAL/binlog -- lowest overhead, used by Debezium); trigger-based CDC (insert/update/delete triggers write to change tables -- higher overhead); and timestamp-based polling (checks updated_at column -- misses deletes). CDC feeds Kafka or data pipelines for real-time analytics, cache invalidation, and data synchronisation."),
    ("What is Debezium?", "Debezium is an open-source CDC platform built on Kafka Connect. It captures row-level changes from MySQL (binlog), PostgreSQL (logical replication), MongoDB (oplog), SQL Server, Oracle, and other databases and publishes them as Kafka events. Enables real-time data synchronisation, event sourcing, cache invalidation, and streaming ETL. Each event contains the before and after state of the row. It is widely used in microservices architectures for the outbox pattern."),
    ("What is database backup and recovery?", "Backup strategies: logical backup (pg_dump -- SQL file, portable, slow); physical/file-system backup (pg_basebackup -- copy of data files, fast); and continuous archiving (WAL archiving to S3 + base backup = PITR). Recovery time objective (RTO): maximum tolerable recovery time. Recovery point objective (RPO): maximum tolerable data loss. For RPO near zero: streaming replication with synchronous standby. Testing restores regularly is as important as taking backups."),
    ("What is point-in-time recovery?", "PITR (Point-in-Time Recovery) allows restoring a database to any moment between backups by replaying WAL archives. Process: restore the base backup (e.g., from S3); then replay WAL files up to the desired target time or LSN. PostgreSQL recovery target options: recovery_target_time, recovery_target_lsn, recovery_target_xid. PITR is essential for recovering from logical errors (accidental DELETE) that replication would propagate. Cloud DBMS (RDS, Cloud SQL) provide PITR as a managed feature."),
    ("What is database high availability?", "High availability (HA) ensures the database remains accessible despite failures. Approaches: primary-standby replication with automatic failover (Patroni for PostgreSQL, MHA for MySQL); shared-nothing clustering (Galera Cluster for MySQL, CockroachDB, YugabyteDB); and managed cloud HA (Amazon RDS Multi-AZ, Aurora, Cloud SQL HA -- fully automated failover in 20-30 seconds). Synchronous replication eliminates data loss; asynchronous allows data loss proportional to lag."),
    ("What is Patroni?", "Patroni is an open-source HA solution for PostgreSQL using etcd, Consul, or ZooKeeper as a distributed consensus store. It monitors primary health, manages leader election, and orchestrates automatic failover to a replica. Configuration is YAML-based. Spilo (Zalando) packages Patroni with PostgreSQL for Kubernetes. Patroni enables zero-configuration failover and is the most popular open-source HA solution for production PostgreSQL deployments."),
    ("What is CockroachDB?", "CockroachDB is a distributed SQL database providing ACID transactions, horizontal scalability, and high availability. It uses the Raft consensus algorithm for replication, a distributed storage layer (RocksDB-based), and a PostgreSQL-compatible SQL dialect. Geographic data distribution (multi-region tables, zone configs) minimises read/write latency. CockroachDB is designed for global applications requiring strong consistency across data centres -- a 'planet-scale SQL' database."),
    ("What is Spanner?", "Google Spanner is a globally distributed, strongly consistent relational database. It achieves external consistency (linearisability) across data centres using TrueTime (GPS + atomic clocks provide bounded clock uncertainty). It supports SQL, schemas, transactions, and scales to billions of rows. Spanner underpins Google Ads, Play, and Search. Cloud Spanner is the managed public offering. Spanner disproves the conventional wisdom that distributed systems must sacrifice consistency for availability."),
    ("What is DynamoDB?", "Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed, serverless key-value and document database offering single-digit millisecond latency at any scale. Data model: tables with items (documents) identified by partition key (and optional sort key). DynamoDB Streams captures item changes for triggers and CDC. Supports on-demand and provisioned capacity. Global Tables provide multi-region active-active replication. DynamoDB accelerator (DAX) is an in-memory cache. Access patterns must be designed upfront (no ad-hoc queries)."),
    ("What is Elasticsearch?", "Elasticsearch is a distributed search and analytics engine built on Apache Lucene. It stores data as JSON documents and provides full-text search, relevance scoring (BM25), aggregations, and geo-queries. Used for: log analytics (ELK stack -- Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana), e-commerce product search, and observability. Inverted index enables sub-second full-text search across billions of documents. Elasticsearch scales horizontally via shards and replicas. Dense vector search (k-NN) is supported for semantic search."),
    ("What is an inverted index?", "An inverted index maps each unique term to the list of documents containing it (and optionally, positions within documents). It enables full-text search: given a query term, instantly retrieve all matching documents. Building: tokenise documents, normalise tokens (lowercasing, stemming, stop words), and store the inverted list. Used in: Elasticsearch/Lucene, PostgreSQL full-text search (GIN index on tsvector), and all search engines. The name 'inverted' contrasts with a forward index (document -> terms)."),
    ("What is full-text search in PostgreSQL?", "PostgreSQL full-text search uses tsvector (preprocessed document) and tsquery (search query) types with the @@ operator. tsvector stores normalised lexemes with positions; to_tsvector('english', text) applies stemming and stop-word removal. GIN indexes on tsvector enable fast full-text search. Ranking: ts_rank() and ts_rank_cd() score results by term frequency and proximity. Suitable for moderate full-text search needs; Elasticsearch is preferable for high-volume or relevance-tuned search."),
    ("What is a GIN index?", "A Generalised Inverted Index (GIN) in PostgreSQL is designed for types where values contain multiple components: full-text search (tsvector), arrays, JSONB, and range types. GIN stores the index entries for each component (lexeme, array element, JSON key/value) with pointers to the rows containing them. Lookups are fast; updates are slower (GIN indexes are expensive to maintain). fastupdate mode batches updates in a pending list, improving write performance at the cost of slower first search after bulk writes."),
    ("What is a GiST index?", "A Generalised Search Tree (GiST) is a flexible index infrastructure in PostgreSQL allowing custom index strategies. It supports: geometric types (PostGIS uses GiST for spatial queries), range types (overlap, contains), full-text search (alternative to GIN), and nearest-neighbour search. GiST is lossy (may return false positives that require heap recheck) but is more update-friendly than GIN. It is also the base for HNSW and IVFFlat vector search indexes in pgvector."),
    ("What is PostGIS?", "PostGIS is a PostgreSQL extension adding spatial data types (geometry, geography), spatial functions (ST_Distance, ST_Within, ST_Intersection, ST_Buffer, ST_Transform), and spatial indexing (GiST). It is the most powerful open-source spatial database, supporting vector (points, lines, polygons), raster data, and 3D geometries. Used in GIS applications, ride-sharing (nearest driver), delivery routing, and geospatial analytics. Compatible with GeoJSON, WKT/WKB, Shapefile, and KML."),
    ("What is database security?", "Database security encompasses: authentication (pg_hba.conf, SSL/TLS client certificates, IAM); authorisation (GRANT/REVOKE privileges on tables, columns, rows; row-level security -- RLS); encryption at rest (LUKS, TDE -- Transparent Data Encryption) and in transit (SSL); auditing (pgaudit extension logs all DDL and DML); SQL injection prevention (parameterised queries -- never interpolate user input into SQL strings); and least-privilege principle (application users should have minimum required permissions)."),
    ("What is SQL injection?", "SQL injection is a critical security vulnerability where user input is unsafely interpolated into SQL strings, allowing attackers to manipulate the query. Example: SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '' OR '1'='1' -- returns all users. Prevention: always use parameterised queries (prepared statements) where user input is passed as a parameter, never concatenated into SQL. ORMs and query builders handle this by default. Never construct SQL strings with string formatting from user input."),
    ("What is row-level security?", "Row-level security (RLS) in PostgreSQL allows policies to restrict which rows a user can see or modify. CREATE POLICY defines a USING expression (for SELECT/DELETE/UPDATE) and WITH CHECK expression (for INSERT/UPDATE) per role. When RLS is enabled on a table, policies filter rows automatically -- the application cannot bypass them. RLS is ideal for multi-tenant applications where each tenant should only see their own data, enforced at the database level."),
    ("What is database monitoring?", "Database monitoring tracks: query performance (slow query log, pg_stat_statements -- query statistics including total calls, total time, rows); lock contention (pg_locks, pg_stat_activity -- long-running transactions holding locks); buffer cache hit rate (pg_statio_user_tables -- heap and index hits/misses); replication lag (pg_stat_replication); table bloat (dead rows from MVCC -- requires VACUUM); and connection counts. Tools: pgBadger (log analyser), pganalyze, Datadog, Prometheus + postgres_exporter."),
    ("What is VACUUM in PostgreSQL?", "PostgreSQL's MVCC model leaves dead row versions (from UPDATE and DELETE) in heap files. VACUUM reclaims dead rows and marks their space as reusable. VACUUM FULL reclaims disk space entirely (rewrites the table -- requires an exclusive lock). AUTOVACUUM runs automatically in the background. Without regular vacuuming, table bloat grows, performance degrades, and transaction ID wraparound can corrupt the database. VACUUM also updates visibility maps and freezes old transaction IDs."),
    ("What is ANALYZE in PostgreSQL?", "ANALYZE collects statistics about the distribution of values in table columns (row count, column histograms, most common values, null fraction) and stores them in pg_statistics. The query planner uses these statistics to estimate the number of rows each plan node will return, informing the choice of join method, index usage, and scan type. Outdated statistics lead to poor plan choices. AUTOVACUUM runs ANALYZE automatically; ANALYZE manually on tables after bulk loads."),
    ("What is pg_stat_statements?", "pg_stat_statements is a PostgreSQL extension that tracks execution statistics for all SQL statements: total calls, total and mean execution time, total rows, shared block hits and reads, and buffer reads. It normalises queries (replaces literal values with $1, $2) to group equivalent queries. Used to identify: the slowest queries (by total time), most frequent queries, and most I/O-intensive queries. Essential for performance tuning -- the first tool to reach for when optimising a PostgreSQL deployment."),
    ("What is logical replication in PostgreSQL?", "Logical replication replicates data at the row level (decoded from WAL) rather than at the physical block level. It allows replication of specific tables (not the entire cluster), across different PostgreSQL major versions, and to external systems. Publications (define what to replicate) and subscriptions (define where to replicate to) are created with CREATE PUBLICATION and CREATE SUBSCRIPTION. Used for: zero-downtime major version upgrades, selective replication, and CDC (via Debezium pgoutput plugin)."),
    ("What is connection management in PostgreSQL?", "Each PostgreSQL connection spawns a dedicated backend process (~5-10 MB of memory). max_connections (default 100) limits total connections. High connection counts (thousands) cause memory pressure and context-switch overhead. Solutions: connection pooling (PgBouncer in transaction mode); pg_bouncer min_pool_size ensures warm connections; and reducing connection lifetime in the application. Cloud databases (Cloud SQL, RDS) support IAM authentication and IAM database authentication to avoid embedding passwords."),
    ("What is a database cursor?", "A cursor is a database object for traversing the results of a query row by row. Useful for large result sets that would exceed memory if fetched all at once. In PostgreSQL: DECLARE cursor_name CURSOR FOR SELECT ...; FETCH n FROM cursor_name; CLOSE cursor_name. Cursors hold a transaction open; use for batch processing of large datasets. SQLAlchemy and psycopg2 support server-side cursors. In application code, prefer streaming queries or pagination over cursors where possible."),
    ("What is database pagination?", "Pagination divides large query results into pages. Offset pagination: LIMIT 20 OFFSET 200 -- simple but slow for large offsets (must scan and discard N rows). Keyset (cursor-based) pagination: WHERE id > last_seen_id ORDER BY id LIMIT 20 -- O(log n) via index, consistent under inserts. Keyset pagination is preferred for large datasets and infinite scroll. Seek method (Markus Winand) generalises keyset to multi-column sort keys."),
    ("What is a sequence in SQL?", "A sequence is a database object that generates unique integer values in order. Used for auto-incrementing primary keys. In PostgreSQL: CREATE SEQUENCE seq_name START 1 INCREMENT 1; SELECT nextval('seq_name'). SERIAL and BIGSERIAL column types automatically create and link a sequence. IDENTITY columns (SQL standard) are preferred over SERIAL in modern PostgreSQL. Sequences are non-transactional -- a rolled-back transaction leaves a gap in the sequence."),
    ("What is UUID as a primary key?", "UUIDs (Universally Unique Identifiers) are 128-bit random identifiers. As primary keys, they: enable distributed key generation without coordination (no single sequence server); allow safe data migration and merging; and expose no information about record count or creation time. Drawbacks: larger than integers (16 vs 4/8 bytes), random insertion causes B-tree page splits and bloat (use UUIDv7 -- time-ordered -- or gen_random_uuid() with fillfactor tuning to mitigate). PostgreSQL supports the uuid type natively."),
    ("What is a JSON column in PostgreSQL?", "PostgreSQL supports json (stored as text, validated) and jsonb (binary format, decomposed and indexed, preferred for querying). JSONB operators: ->'key' (extract JSON), ->>'key' (extract as text), @> (containment -- is right a subset of left?), ? (key exists). GIN indexes on JSONB columns support fast containment and key-exists queries. JSONB combines relational structure with document flexibility -- eliminating the need for a separate document store for many use cases."),
    ("What is a database transaction log?", "The transaction log (WAL in PostgreSQL, redo log in InnoDB/MySQL, transaction log in SQL Server) is a sequential, append-only log of all changes made to the database, written before data pages are modified (write-ahead). It is the foundation of: crash recovery (replay uncommitted changes after crash); replication (stream WAL to replicas); PITR (apply WAL from archived WAL segments); and CDC (read WAL for change events). The transaction log is the single source of truth for the committed state of the database."),
    ("What is database denormalisation?", "Denormalisation deliberately introduces redundancy into a schema to improve read performance. Techniques: duplicating columns in child tables to avoid JOINs; pre-computing aggregations (cached_count columns); materialised views; and vertical splitting (separating hot and cold columns into different tables). Denormalisation trades write overhead and consistency complexity for read speed. It is appropriate in: OLAP schemas (star schema), read-heavy APIs with well-defined access patterns, and caching layers."),
    ("What is the difference between DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP?", "DELETE removes rows one at a time, fires triggers, is fully transactional (can be rolled back), and logs each row deletion. Slow for large tables. TRUNCATE removes all rows instantly by deallocating pages, does not fire row-level triggers, is transactional in PostgreSQL but not MySQL (MyISAM), and is much faster than DELETE for clearing a table. DROP removes the entire table structure and all data permanently and is not reversible (without a backup)."),
    ("What is optimistic vs pessimistic locking?", "Pessimistic locking acquires a lock on a row before reading it (SELECT FOR UPDATE), preventing concurrent modifications. Safe but reduces concurrency and risks deadlocks. Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare: read a version number, perform the update conditional on the version being unchanged (UPDATE ... WHERE version = N AND id = X), and retry on conflict. Optimistic locking is preferred in high-read, low-conflict scenarios (e-commerce, booking) -- it avoids long-held locks at the cost of occasional retry overhead."),
    ("What is SELECT FOR UPDATE?", "SELECT FOR UPDATE acquires a row-level exclusive lock on the selected rows, preventing other transactions from locking or modifying them until the current transaction commits or rolls back. Used for: read-modify-write cycles where the read value informs the write (preventing lost updates). SKIP LOCKED skips rows already locked (useful for job queues -- each worker grabs unlocked jobs). NOWAIT returns an error immediately instead of waiting if the lock is unavailable."),
    ("What is a materialized view?", "A materialised view stores the result of a query physically on disk. Unlike regular views (which re-run the query each time), materialised views are fast to query because the result is precomputed. They must be refreshed to reflect changes in base tables: REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW name (blocks reads); REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY (non-blocking -- requires a unique index). Suitable for expensive aggregation queries used by dashboards or reports where some staleness is acceptable."),
    ("What is a database schema migration?", "A schema migration is a versioned, controlled change to the database schema (adding/dropping columns, creating tables, changing constraints). Migration tools: Flyway (Java -- SQL scripts named V1__description.sql); Liquibase (XML/YAML/SQL changesets); Alembic (Python/SQLAlchemy); Prisma Migrate (TypeScript); and goose (Go). Best practices: migrations are one-directional and versioned; tested in staging before production; and applied automatically in CI/CD pipelines. Backward-compatible migrations (expand-contract pattern) avoid downtime."),
    ("What is the expand-contract migration pattern?", "The expand-contract pattern enables zero-downtime schema migrations: Expand: add the new column or table alongside the old (both old and new code work). Migrate: backfill data from old to new (can be done online). Contract: once all application versions have been deployed and use the new schema, drop the old column/table. This avoids the deployment-database-migration synchronisation problem where a new schema breaks old code or vice versa."),
    ("What is a hot standby?", "A hot standby is a read-only replica that is continuously receiving WAL from the primary and can serve SELECT queries. It provides read scaling (route read-heavy queries to replicas) and a warm failover target. In PostgreSQL, hot standby is enabled with hot_standby = on in postgresql.conf. Replication lag (the delay between primary commit and replica visibility) should be monitored (pg_stat_replication, pg_replication_slots). Synchronous replication eliminates lag but adds commit latency."),
    ("What is a replication slot?", "A PostgreSQL replication slot tracks the WAL position consumed by a replica or logical replication consumer, preventing the primary from recycling WAL segments needed by a lagging consumer. Slots ensure no data is lost even if a replica disconnects temporarily. Risk: if a replica with a slot is gone for a long time, WAL accumulates and may fill the disk. Monitor slot lag (pg_replication_slots.confirmed_flush_lsn vs pg_current_wal_lsn). Set a max_slot_wal_keep_size limit to prevent disk exhaustion."),
    ("What is database as a service?", "DBaaS (Database as a Service) provides managed database hosting: the cloud provider handles provisioning, patching, backups, high availability, and monitoring. Examples: Amazon RDS/Aurora, Google Cloud SQL/AlloyDB, Azure Database for PostgreSQL, Neon (serverless PostgreSQL), PlanetScale (serverless MySQL/Vitess), Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage). DBaaS trades control and customisation for reduced operational overhead. Suitable for most applications; self-managed databases are preferred for extreme performance tuning or compliance requirements."),
    ("What is Aurora?", "Amazon Aurora is a cloud-native relational database compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, offering 3-5x performance improvement. Its distributed storage layer (6 copies across 3 AZs, redo log shipping to storage tier) decouples compute from storage. Aurora Serverless v2 automatically scales compute capacity. Aurora Global Database replicates across regions with <1s lag. Aurora eliminates traditional I/O bottlenecks -- storage nodes apply redo log updates, never shipping dirty pages."),
    ("What is Supabase?", "Supabase is an open-source Firebase alternative built on PostgreSQL. It provides: a managed PostgreSQL database; instant REST and GraphQL APIs (PostgREST); real-time subscriptions (via PostgreSQL logical replication); authentication (JWT + row-level security); edge functions; and object storage. Supabase enables backend-free application development with PostgreSQL's full power. It is production-ready and deployable self-hosted or on Supabase Cloud."),
    ("What is PlanetScale?", "PlanetScale is a serverless MySQL database platform built on Vitess (YouTube's MySQL sharding middleware). Features: branching workflows (database branches like Git branches for schema changes -- no locking migrations); non-blocking schema changes (using gh-ost); horizontal sharding via Vitess; automatic scaling; and a powerful query insights dashboard. It is particularly popular in the JavaScript ecosystem (Next.js, Prisma ORM)."),
    ("What is Vitess?", "Vitess is an open-source database clustering system for MySQL providing horizontal sharding, connection pooling (VTGate), and query routing. Developed at YouTube to scale MySQL to billions of queries per day. Features: transparent sharding (application connects to VTGate as if to a single MySQL); resharding without downtime; connection multiplexing (VTTablet pools); and consistent metadata (via Topology Service using etcd or ZooKeeper). Used by Slack, GitHub, HubSpot, and PlanetScale."),
    ("What is ClickHouse?", "ClickHouse is an open-source column-oriented DBMS for real-time analytical queries. It achieves extreme query performance (billions of rows per second) via: columnar storage, vectorised execution (SIMD operations on column batches), aggressive compression (LZ4, ZSTD), and a shared-nothing architecture. Supports SQL dialect. Weaknesses: poor support for transactions, limited UPDATE/DELETE performance (designed for append-only workloads), and complex cluster management. Used by: Cloudflare, Yandex, ByteDance, and PostHog."),
    ("What is DuckDB?", "DuckDB is an in-process analytical SQL database (like SQLite but for OLAP). It runs entirely within the application process (no server) and queries Parquet, CSV, and JSON files directly from disk or S3 without loading them. Columnar vectorised execution enables sub-second queries over millions of rows on a laptop. Used for: local data exploration, ETL scripts, and embedded analytics in Python data pipelines. DuckDB bridges the gap between Pandas (memory-limited) and cloud data warehouses (latency and cost)."),
    ("What is the difference between truncate and delete in SQL?", "TRUNCATE instantly removes all rows by deallocating pages, cannot be filtered (WHERE clause is not supported), does not fire row-level triggers, resets sequences in some DBMS, is DDL (auto-commits in MySQL), and is much faster for large tables. DELETE removes rows one by one, supports WHERE filtering, fires triggers, is fully transactional (can be rolled back), and generates WAL for each row. Use TRUNCATE to empty a table completely; DELETE to remove specific rows."),
    ("What is EXPLAIN ANALYZE?", "EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes a query and displays the actual execution plan with real timings and row counts alongside the planner's estimates. Key information: node type (Seq Scan, Index Scan, Hash Join), actual vs estimated rows (large discrepancies indicate stale statistics), actual time per node, buffer hits and reads (I/O cost), and parallelism. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, FORMAT JSON) provides the richest output. Identify performance bottlenecks: sequential scans on large tables, nested loops over large sets, and sort operations."),
    ("What is an execution plan cache?", "Most databases cache query execution plans (prepared statement plan cache) to avoid re-planning identical queries. PostgreSQL caches plans for prepared statements after 5 executions (generic plan threshold). SQL Server uses a plan cache (sys.dm_exec_cached_plans). Plan cache bloat (many single-use plans) is a common cause of memory pressure. Parameterised queries (bind variables) promote plan reuse. Adaptive query execution (Spark, SQL Server) re-optimises plans at runtime based on actual row counts."),
    ("What is a self-join?", "A self-join joins a table to itself, typically using different aliases. Common uses: finding hierarchical relationships within a single table (employees and their managers in the same table -- JOIN employees AS e ON e.manager_id = mgr.employee_id); comparing rows within the same table (finding pairs with certain relationships); and detecting duplicates. Self-joins are logically identical to other joins but require careful aliasing to distinguish the two references to the same table."),
    ("What is COALESCE in SQL?", "COALESCE(a, b, c, ...) returns the first non-NULL argument in the list. Used to: substitute default values for NULLs (COALESCE(discount, 0)); provide fallback column values; and conditionally compute expressions. Equivalent to CASE WHEN a IS NOT NULL THEN a WHEN b IS NOT NULL THEN b ELSE c END. NULLIF(a, b) is the inverse -- returns NULL if a = b, else a. These functions handle NULL arithmetic (any arithmetic with NULL returns NULL) and comparisons (NULL is not equal to anything, including NULL)."),
    ("What is a lateral join?", "A LATERAL join (supported in PostgreSQL, MySQL 8+, SQL Server as CROSS APPLY) allows a subquery in the FROM clause to reference columns from preceding tables in the same FROM clause -- like a correlated subquery but returning multiple rows. Example: SELECT c.name, o.* FROM customers c LEFT JOIN LATERAL (SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = c.id ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 3) o ON true -- returns the 3 most recent orders per customer. LATERAL enables per-row subqueries returning sets."),
    ("What is UPSERT?", "UPSERT inserts a row if it does not exist, or updates it if it does, in a single atomic operation -- avoiding race conditions. PostgreSQL: INSERT INTO ... ON CONFLICT (key_column) DO UPDATE SET col = EXCLUDED.col. MySQL: INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE or REPLACE INTO. SQL standard: MERGE statement. UPSERT is essential for idempotent writes, cache invalidation, and syncing data from external systems. It is more efficient than a separate SELECT + INSERT/UPDATE and avoids lost update races."),
    ("What is a database bloom filter?", "PostgreSQL's bloom filter extension (pg_trgm and bloom) provides a multi-column index using a probabilistic bloom filter data structure. It supports equality queries across many columns efficiently (each column gets a few bits in a bloom filter per row), unlike a B-tree (which only efficiently supports prefix queries). Useful for tables with many columns that are queried in arbitrary combinations. Space-efficient but probabilistic -- may have false positives (requiring heap recheck) but no false negatives."),
    ("What is BRIN index?", "BRIN (Block Range Index) stores min and max values of a column for each range of physical pages (block range). Extremely small (kilobytes vs gigabytes for B-tree on large tables) and suitable for naturally ordered data (creation timestamps in append-only tables). Queries on the indexed column can skip ranges whose min-max does not overlap the query predicate. BRIN is ideal for very large tables where the data is physically sorted (e.g., time-series tables loaded chronologically)."),
    ("What is database compression?", "Databases use multiple compression strategies: row-level TOAST compression (PostgreSQL compresses large column values -- JSON, text -- when they exceed ~2KB); column-level compression in columnar stores (run-length encoding for low-cardinality columns, dictionary encoding for strings, delta encoding for timestamps, bitpacking for small integers); and file-level compression (zstd, lz4 on Parquet/ORC files, PostgreSQL table-level compression via pg_lzcompress). Compression reduces I/O (the bottleneck for analytical queries) at the cost of CPU."),
    ("What is schema-on-read vs schema-on-write?", "Schema-on-write (relational databases): the schema is defined before data is loaded; data must conform to the schema on write. Provides strong consistency and fast reads. Schema-on-read (data lakes, document databases): raw data is stored without schema enforcement; the schema is applied at query time. Provides flexibility (can store data before knowing how it will be queried) at the cost of data quality risk and slower reads (no pre-validation). Lakehouse architectures blend both: schema-on-write for curated layers, schema-on-read for raw ingestion."),
    ("What is data governance?", "Data governance is the set of policies, processes, and standards ensuring data quality, security, privacy, and compliance. Components: data catalogue (metadata -- what data exists, where, who owns it -- Apache Atlas, DataHub, Alation); data lineage (how data flows and transforms -- dbt lineage graphs, Marquez); data quality (completeness, accuracy, consistency -- Great Expectations, Monte Carlo); data access control (RBAC, RLS, column masking); and compliance (GDPR data retention and deletion, HIPAA PHI access logging)."),
    ("What is a slowly changing dimension?", "A slowly changing dimension (SCD) tracks changes to dimension attributes over time in a data warehouse. Types: SCD Type 1 (overwrite old value -- no history); SCD Type 2 (add new row with effective date range and is_current flag -- full history, most common); SCD Type 3 (add a previous_value column -- limited history); SCD Type 6 (hybrid of 1, 2, 3). SCD Type 2 enables historical analysis ('what was the customer's address at the time of the order?') but increases dimension table size."),
    ("What is a database ORM N+1 solution?", "Solutions to the N+1 query problem in ORMs: eager loading (SQLAlchemy: options(joinedload(Order.customer)) -- JOIN in a single query); batch loading (DataLoader pattern -- collect all IDs in a batch, execute one SELECT IN query); explicit JOINs in ORM query (ORM with raw join hints); or drop down to raw SQL for complex queries. Monitoring N+1 queries: Django Debug Toolbar, SQLAlchemy query logging, and query count assertions in tests."),
    ("What is database observability?", "Database observability goes beyond monitoring metrics to understanding query behaviour, cardinality, and system internals. Tools: pg_stat_statements (query statistics); auto_explain (log slow query plans automatically); pg_activity (real-time activity viewer like htop for PostgreSQL); pgBadger (log analyser generating HTML reports); pganalyze (SaaS deep observability for PostgreSQL -- index advisor, wait event analysis, VACUUM advisor); and Datadog/Prometheus/Grafana for metrics and alerting on lag, connections, and cache hit rate."),
    ("What is database federation?", "Database federation creates a unified query interface over multiple heterogeneous data sources. PostgreSQL Foreign Data Wrappers (FDW) allow querying external data (other PostgreSQL servers, MySQL, Redis, S3/Parquet, REST APIs) as if they were local tables. Examples: postgres_fdw (remote PostgreSQL), mysql_fdw, redis_fdw, parquet_fdw. Federation enables JOIN across data sources without ETL. Performance depends on predicate pushdown to the remote source -- not all FDWs support it."),
    ("What is the two-phase commit?", "Two-phase commit (2PC) is a distributed transaction protocol ensuring atomicity across multiple databases. Phase 1 (Prepare): the coordinator asks all participants to prepare to commit and promise not to abort. Phase 2 (Commit/Abort): if all participants ACK Prepare, the coordinator sends Commit; if any fail, it sends Abort. 2PC is blocking -- if the coordinator fails after Prepare but before Commit, participants are locked. PostgreSQL supports 2PC via PREPARE TRANSACTION and COMMIT PREPARED. Saga pattern is a more resilient alternative for microservices."),
    ("What is the Saga pattern?", "The Saga pattern manages distributed transactions across microservices by breaking a long-running transaction into a sequence of local transactions, each publishing an event or message. If a step fails, compensating transactions undo the prior steps. Types: choreography-based (each service listens for events and acts); orchestration-based (a central saga orchestrator tells each service what to do). The Saga pattern achieves eventual consistency without distributed locks, suitable for microservices architectures where 2PC is impractical."),
    ("What is database benchmarking?", "Database benchmarking measures performance under controlled workloads. Standard benchmarks: TPC-C (OLTP -- order processing, a mix of transactions); TPC-H (analytical -- decision support queries over lineitem tables); TPC-DS (complex analytics -- 100 queries); and YCSB (Yahoo Cloud Serving Benchmark -- key-value and document workloads). Tools: pgbench (PostgreSQL's built-in TPC-B-like benchmark), sysbench (MySQL/PostgreSQL), HammerDB. Benchmarks should reflect production access patterns for meaningful results."),
    ("What is a surrogate key?", "A surrogate key is a system-generated artificial primary key (auto-increment integer or UUID) with no business meaning. It is preferred over natural keys (email, SSN, ISBN) because: natural keys can change (email address updates break child table references); natural keys may be long strings (wider indexes, larger foreign keys); and surrogate keys provide a stable identity that does not depend on external systems. The trade-off: surrogate keys require an additional JOIN to look up the natural key in business queries."),
    ("What is database normalisation vs denormalisation trade-offs?", "Normalisation reduces redundancy, update anomalies, and storage; it ensures data integrity but requires JOINs for most queries. Denormalisation duplicates data to reduce JOINs and improve read performance, at the cost of: write overhead (update all copies); consistency risk (copies can diverge); and increased storage. Practical strategy: normalise the OLTP schema (3NF) for data integrity; denormalise the analytical schema (star schema) for query performance. Caching layers and materialised views provide intermediate solutions."),
    ("What is a database constraint?", "Constraints enforce data integrity rules at the database level. Types: PRIMARY KEY (unique + not null); UNIQUE (no duplicate values -- allows NULLs); NOT NULL (column cannot be null); CHECK (boolean expression -- e.g., price > 0); FOREIGN KEY (referential integrity); EXCLUSION (no two rows can overlap on specified attributes -- PostgreSQL, used in PostGIS and scheduling). Constraints are enforced on every write, catching data integrity violations before they corrupt the database."),
    ("What is a partial index?", "A partial index (PostgreSQL) indexes only rows satisfying a WHERE condition, producing a smaller, more selective index. Example: CREATE INDEX ON orders (created_at) WHERE status = 'pending' -- only indexes pending orders, not the millions of completed ones. This allows the index to fit in cache, reduces maintenance overhead, and improves query performance for queries that also filter on the indexed condition. Useful for: soft deletes (WHERE deleted_at IS NULL), active records, and sparse categorical columns."),
    ("What is online schema change?", "Online schema changes (OSC) modify schema without locking the table. Tools: pt-online-schema-change (pt-osc -- MySQL, creates a shadow table and copies data); gh-ost (GitHub, uses MySQL binlog to apply changes to a shadow table without triggers); and pg_repack (PostgreSQL, rebuilds the table online). Modern PostgreSQL supports many DDL operations as non-blocking (ADD COLUMN with a default -- since v11, uses a table rewrite only for volatile defaults; CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY -- no exclusive lock). PlanetScale builds non-blocking migrations into the platform."),
    ("What is a database proxy?", "A database proxy sits between applications and databases, providing: connection pooling (PgBouncer, ProxySQL); query routing (read/write splitting -- writes to primary, reads to replicas); failover (redirect connections after primary failure); query firewall (block destructive queries); and caching. AWS RDS Proxy, Google Cloud SQL Auth Proxy, and PlanetScale's edge infrastructure provide managed proxy functionality. Proxies add a network hop but the benefits typically outweigh the latency cost."),
    ("What is read replica?", "A read replica is a database copy that receives replication updates from the primary and serves read-only queries. Benefits: offload read-heavy queries from the primary; scale read throughput by adding replicas; and provide a failover target. Limitations: replication lag means reads may be stale; replicas cannot accept writes; and the primary is a single point of write failure. Cloud DBMS provide read replicas as a managed feature (RDS read replica, Cloud SQL read replica). The application must route reads to replicas and writes to the primary."),
    ("What is database cost optimisation?", "Database cost optimisation in cloud environments: right-size instances (avoid over-provisioning -- use monitoring to find actual peak utilisation); use reserved instances (1-3 year commitments give 30-60% discount over on-demand); archive or delete old data (S3 Glacier for cold data); use serverless databases (Aurora Serverless, Neon -- scale to zero when idle); columnar compression (Parquet on S3 is ~10x cheaper than RDS for analytical data); read replicas to scale reads without upgrading the primary; and connection pooling to reduce connection overhead."),
    ("What is a recursive CTE?", "A recursive CTE uses WITH RECURSIVE to traverse hierarchical or graph-structured data. Structure: anchor member (initial query, no self-reference) UNION ALL recursive member (references the CTE itself). Example: traversing an organisational hierarchy -- anchor fetches the root employee; recursive member JOINs employees to the CTE on manager_id to find all subordinates. PostgreSQL adds CYCLE detection and DEPTH tracking (WITH RECURSIVE ... SEARCH DEPTH FIRST / BREADTH FIRST). Use cases: org charts, bill of materials, graph path-finding."),
    ("What is a foreign data wrapper?", "A PostgreSQL Foreign Data Wrapper (FDW) is an extension that implements the SQL/MED standard to query external data sources as if they were local PostgreSQL tables. The FDW API defines scan, insert, update, and delete callbacks. Examples: postgres_fdw (query remote PostgreSQL), file_fdw (query CSV files), mysql_fdw, redis_fdw, parquet_fdw, and multicorn (Python FDW framework). FDWs support predicate pushdown (sending WHERE clauses to the remote source to minimise data transfer)."),
    ("What is database audit logging?", "Audit logging records who accessed or modified what data and when, for compliance (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX, PCI-DSS) and security. PostgreSQL: pgaudit extension logs DDL (CREATE, DROP), DML (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), and privilege events to the PostgreSQL log. MySQL: audit log plugin. Cloud DBMS: CloudWatch Logs (RDS), Cloud Logging (Cloud SQL). Audit logs should be: immutable, stored separately from the database, retained per compliance requirements (typically 1-7 years), and monitored for anomalous access patterns."),
    ("What is database encryption?", "Encryption at rest protects data files if storage media is stolen. Methods: OS-level full-disk encryption (LUKS/dm-crypt); file-system encryption (ZFS encryption); cloud provider managed encryption (AES-256, KMS key management -- default in RDS, Cloud SQL, BigQuery); and TDE (Transparent Data Encryption -- SQL Server, Oracle, MySQL Enterprise). Encryption in transit uses TLS (ssl = on in postgresql.conf; verify-full SSL mode in client connections -- certificate validation prevents MITM attacks)."),
    ("What is the database buffer pool?", "The buffer pool (PostgreSQL: shared_buffers) is an in-memory cache of database pages (8KB blocks). Frequently accessed pages are kept in the buffer pool, avoiding disk I/O (physical reads). Buffer cache hit rate = (heap_blks_hit / (heap_blks_hit + heap_blks_read)) should be > 99% for OLTP workloads. Size recommendation: 25% of RAM for PostgreSQL shared_buffers, with the OS page cache handling the rest. InnoDB buffer pool size (MySQL) should be 50-80% of RAM on dedicated database servers."),
    ("What is a database advisory lock?", "Advisory locks (PostgreSQL) are application-defined locks not tied to any specific table or row. They are cooperative -- the DBMS enforces them only if the application explicitly acquires them. Functions: pg_advisory_lock(key) (session-level, held until released or session ends); pg_advisory_xact_lock(key) (transaction-level, released on COMMIT/ROLLBACK); pg_try_advisory_lock(key) (non-blocking). Uses: distributed leader election, preventing duplicate job execution in background workers, and mutual exclusion for application-level operations."),
    ("What is the MVCC vacuum overhead?", "PostgreSQL's MVCC model accumulates dead row versions (tuples) from UPDATEs and DELETEs. These dead tuples: waste disk space (table bloat); slow sequential scans (must visit and skip dead tuples); and eventually cause transaction ID (XID) wraparound if not frozen (catastrophic). VACUUM reclaims dead tuples and freezes old XIDs. AUTOVACUUM runs automatically but may not keep up with high-write workloads -- monitor pg_stat_user_tables.n_dead_tup and autovacuum_count. Manual VACUUM with custom parameters (cost_delay, cost_page_hit) can be run during low-traffic windows."),
    ("What is database high availability patterns?", "HA patterns: Primary-standby (single primary, async/sync replicas, automatic failover via Patroni/MHA); Multi-master (all nodes accept writes, conflict resolution required -- Galera, CockroachDB, YugabyteDB); Active-active (traffic distributed across nodes -- requires conflict-free operations or careful partitioning); and Leader-follower with consensus (Raft/Paxos for automatic leader election -- etcd, CockroachDB). Cloud managed HA (RDS Multi-AZ) uses synchronous replication and automated failover within the same region (typically <30 seconds)."),
]

